Good — this is a clear starting point. Below I’ll (1) confirm the current financial picture from the numbers you gave, (2) show quick scenario math so you can see the impact of cuts or revenue moves on runway, (3) give prioritized, practical margin-improvement and scalability actions, and (4) give a tight finance narrative / slide outline you can use in internal materials.

1) Current snapshot (from your numbers)
- Cash: $1.2M
- Runway: ~6.15 months (1.2M ÷ $195K)
- Net burn: $195K / month (cash consumed after revenue)
- Revenue (last quarter): $1.8M → avg $600K/month
- Implied monthly operating spend: revenue + burn = $600K + $195K = $795K

Notes: I’m assuming “burn rate” is net cash burn (expenses minus revenue). If you use a different definition, tell me and I’ll adjust.

2) Quick scenario math — runway impact
(Starting point: cash $1.2M, revenue $600K/mo, expenses $795K/mo)

- No change: burn $195K → runway ≈ 6.15 months
- 10% expense reduction (expenses → $715.5K): burn $115.5K → runway ≈ 10.4 months
- 20% expense reduction (expenses → $636K): burn $36K → runway ≈ 33.3 months
- 10% revenue growth (revenue → $660K): burn $135K → runway ≈ 8.9 months
- 20% revenue growth (revenue → $720K): burn $75K → runway ≈ 16.0 months
- Combined 10% revenue growth + 10% expense cut: burn $55.5K → runway ≈ 21.6 months

Takeaway: modest revenue growth or modest cost reductions individually materially extend runway; combined moves are powerful. Translating strategy into either growth or cost action is your lever.

3) Practical levers for margin improvement (prioritized)
Immediate (0–30 days — focus on runway extension)
- Freeze non-critical hiring and pause new projects/capex.
- Cash management: tighten AR (accelerate collections), extend AP where feasible, delay vendor payments with negotiation.
- Cut low-ROI spend (marketing channels, consulting, events) and reallocate to highest ROI channels.
- renegotiate vendor contracts and SaaS licenses; look for near-term credits or shorter-term cancelation rights.
- Run a headcount / role-priority review: identify roles that drive revenue vs overhead and triage accordingly.

Near term (30–90 days — stop the bleeding + quick wins)
- Product / pricing: increase prices or introduce premium tiers where the market supports it; test price increases on a subset of customers.
- SKU / customer rationalization: focus on highest-margin products and clients (stop-sale/discounts for unprofitable segments).
- Reduce COGS / direct costs: renegotiate supplier terms, optimize fulfillment, consolidate vendors.
- Marketing & Sales efficiency: shift spend to channels with best CAC payback; improve conversion funnels.
- Automation & outsourcing: shift repetitive work to automation/contractors to reduce FTE costs.

Medium term (3–12 months — structural scalability)
- Move revenue mix to more recurring, higher-margin offerings (subscriptions, services with retainers).
- Platformize or productize bespoke services to raise gross margins.
- Invest in tools/processes that deliver operating leverage (customer self-service, automated billing, data analytics).
- Revise compensation to tie more to revenue (variable comp, commission) to improve scalability.
- Evaluate strategic M&A or partnerships to quickly scale profitable revenue channels.

4) Scalability-specific recommendations
- Measure unit economics (contribution margin per customer / gross margin per product). Only scale channels with positive contribution payback.
- Track CAC payback period and LTV:CAC by cohort. Aim for payback <12 months where possible.
- Create a clear SLA / onboarding funnel that reduces time-to-value for customers (improves conversion, reduces churn).
- Standardize processes and KPIs across teams to enable repeatable scale (onboarding, support, sales ops).
- Use cloud & SaaS cost governance to ensure variable costs scale sub-linearly vs revenue.

5) KPIs to include in your finance narrative
- Cash balance, runway (months)
- Monthly net burn and burn composition (fixed vs variable)
- Monthly revenue (MRR if applicable) and QoQ growth %
- Gross margin % by product line
- Contribution margin per customer / cohort
- CAC, CAC payback, LTV, LTV:CAC
- Churn and retention metrics
- Operating leverage indicators (revenue per FTE, gross margin per FTE)
- Top 5 fixed cost buckets (payroll, rent, marketing, software, contractors)

6) Suggested concise finance narrative (two-paragraph version for execs/investors)
- Paragraph 1 (current state): “Horizon Media enters the period with $1.2M cash and a ~6-month runway. Last quarter revenue averaged $600K/month, but net cash burn remains ~$195K/month driven by [insert top cost drivers — e.g., fixed payroll and vendor commitments].”
- Paragraph 2 (plan & targets): “Our focus is twofold: (A) protect runway through immediate cost discipline and cash optimization, and (B) reaccelerate high-margin revenue growth. Near-term targets: extend runway to 9–12+ months within 90 days via a 10–15% reduction in controllable spend and monetization/pricing actions that target 10–20% revenue upside on priority channels. Medium-term objective: improve contribution margins and achieve positive operating cash flow within X quarters by productizing services and investing in automation that yields operating leverage.”

7) Slide / one-pager structure (recommended)
- Slide 1: Executive summary — Cash, Runway, Burn, Revenue, One-sentence plan
- Slide 2: How we got here — revenue mix, cost drivers (chart), 3 biggest risks
- Slide 3: Scenarios — current, cost-cut, revenue-growth, combined (table with runway numbers)
- Slide 4: 90-day action plan — bullets, owners, expected cash / margin impact
- Slide 5: 6–12 month roadmap — product/scale initiatives, KPI targets
- Appendix: detailed cash flow and sensitivity assumptions

8) Next steps / how I can help
- I can draft the one-page narrative or the five-slide deck text (you can paste it into your slide template).
- I can build a simple scenario model (Excel-style) with the sensitivity table above so you can tweak assumptions.
- To tailor more precisely I’ll need: gross margin by product, headcount and payroll, top fixed costs, AR/AP days, and whether you plan to fundraise.

Which deliverable would you like first — a concise one-page narrative, a short slide deck text, or a simple scenario model you can tweak? Also tell me any constraints (target runway you want to achieve, whether layoffs are off-limits, and if you’re planning a fundraising round).

