Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — this is a good starting point. Below I’ve put together (1) a quick financial read of where you are, (2) prioritized margin-improvement & scalability levers, (3) short-term action plan with estimated impact (using clear assumptions), (4) the finance narrative / slide outline you can use in an internal presentation, and (5) next steps and data I’d need to build a one-page model or slides.

1) Quick baseline (from your numbers)
- Cash: $1.2M
- Runway: 6 months (you stated this; matches calculation below)
- Burn rate (net cash burn): $195K / month
- Last quarter revenue: $1.8M → average monthly revenue = $600K
- Implied monthly operating expenses = revenue + burn = 600K + 195K = 795K

This means you’re spending ~$795K/month to generate $600K/month in revenue, producing a net cash outflow of $195K/month.

2) Primary levers to improve margin and scale
Revenue-side (increase top line and unit economics)
- Pricing and packaging: modest price increases, higher-tier packaging, or value-based pricing for enterprise customers.
- Upsell / cross-sell: increase average revenue per customer (ARPU).
- Sales focus shift: prioritize low-CAC, high-LTV segments (enterprise, retention, multi-month commitments).
- Productize services / create recurring bundles to improve predictability.

Cost-side (reduce cash burn and fixed costs)
- Immediate cost reductions: hiring freeze, non-essential spend pause, vendor renegotiation, travel & events cuts.
- Right-size org: evaluate roles with low ROI; consider attrition, natural churn, or targeted reductions.
- Outsource / automation: move repeatable operational work to cheaper providers or automate (cloud, billing, support tools).
- Procurement & SRE cost saves: renegotiate cloud, license and vendor contracts.

Capital / financing options
- Customer prepayments, discounted annual contracts.
- Short-term bridge financing or revenue-based financing.
- Delayed capex or project deferrals.

Operational scalability levers
- Improve gross margin per unit (reduce variable costs).
- Standardize onboarding and delivery to reduce per-customer cost.
- Invest in scalable channels (self-serve, partnerships, API/ integrations) instead of headcount-heavy sales.

3) Impact scenarios (illustrative, transparent assumptions)
Assumptions:
- Current monthly revenue = $600K
- Current monthly expenses = $795K
- Current burn = 195K
- Cash = $1.2M

Scenario A — Immediate 15% expense reduction (non-revenue-impacting items)
- New expenses = 795K * 0.85 = 675.75K
- New burn = 675.75K - 600K = 75.75K
- New runway = 1.2M / 75.75K ≈ 15.9 months
Impact: runway from 6 → ~16 months. Big leverage from a modest cut when expenses are larger than revenue.

Scenario B — 20% revenue growth (pricing/upsell/new sales) with expenses unchanged
- New revenue = 600K * 1.20 = 720K
- New burn = 795K - 720K = 75K
- New runway ≈ 1.2M / 75K = 16 months
Impact: similar runway extension as Scenario A — shows revenue and cost levers are both powerful.

Scenario C — Combine moderate revenue growth (+10%) and modest expense reduction (-10%)
- Revenue = 660K; Expenses = 715.5K → Burn = 55.5K → Runway ≈ 21.6 months
Impact: combined improvements quickly flip to cash-positive or minimal burn.

Scenario D — Small cuts only (5% expense reduction)
- Expenses = 755.25K → Burn = 155.25K → Runway ≈ 7.7 months
Impact: small improvements give limited runway extension — might not be sufficient.

Takeaway: relatively modest changes to either revenue (10–20%) or costs (10–15%) materially change runway. Prioritize actions that have fast, predictable cash impact.

4) Prioritized 90-day action plan (what to do now, and why)
0–30 days (immediate stop-gap)
- Freeze hires and non-essential spend; pause discretionary projects.
- Vendor and cloud cost reviews: immediate renegotiation or temporary reductions.
- Sales: push for annual prepayments and renewals; prioritize highest ARPU deals.
- Finance: tighten cash approval thresholds; reforecast weekly and present to execs.
Expected result: quick reduction in cash outflows and small revenue uplifts (prepayments).

30–90 days (medium term)
- Implement pricing changes for new deals and at renewal; launch targeted upsell campaigns.
- Evaluate org and staffing to remove low-ROI roles or consolidate functions.
- Automate manual processes (billing, onboarding) to reduce per-customer costs.
- Create “essential” vs “nice-to-have” project list; defer low-value projects.
Expected result: structural lowering of operating cost base, better unit economics.

90–180 days (scale & durable improvements)
- Productize services and build self-service flows to lower CAC.
- Launch channel partnerships and enterprise plays for scalable growth.
- Measure LTV / CAC and invest only where LTV/CAC > 3:1.
Expected result: improved gross margin and scalable growth engine.

5) KPIs to track weekly / monthly
- Cash on hand and runway (weeks/months)
- Net burn (cash outflow per month)
- Monthly recurring revenue (MRR) / revenue growth rate
- Gross margin and gross margin % (by product)
- Contribution margin per customer / cohort
- CAC, LTV, LTV/CAC
- Churn (revenue and customer)
- Days sales outstanding (DSO), AR aging
- Headcount / revenue ratio

6) Finance narrative and slide outline (short version you can present)
Executive summary slide (one-sentence problem + 1-sentence ask)
- Example: “Horizon Media has $1.2M cash and ~6 months runway. We must extend runway and improve margins by focusing on quick cost reductions and revenue initiatives. Proposed ask: approve 90-day cost-control package + permission to implement pricing & prepayment incentives.”

Current state
- Key facts: cash, runway, monthly revenue, monthly expenses, burn
- One chart: cash balance runway and burn waterfall

Drivers of near-term risk
- Top 3 cost drivers (headcount, vendor/cloud, marketing)
- Top 3 revenue risks (renewals, churn, pipeline)

Prioritized plan (0–30 / 30–90 / 90–180)
- Actions, owner, timeline, expected $ impact (conservative / likely / upside)

Scenario outcomes
- Show A/B/C scenarios above with runway and cash balances

KPIs & governance
- Weekly cash review, reforecast cadence, decision triggers (e.g., runway < 4 months triggers X)

Ask / decisions required
- Hiring freeze, approval to renegotiate contracts, authority to change pricing, target cost-reduction percentage

Appendix: detailed P&L and assumptions

7) Suggested CFO talking points / narrative
- “We have clear visibility: $1.2M cash and a 6-month runway at current cadence. That means we need to take actions now to avoid painful decisions later.”
- “Three levers: grow predictable revenue, raise unit economics via pricing & upsells, and reduce fixed/variable costs. Each lever by itself can materially extend runway — combined they will put us on a sustainable path.”
- “We propose a 90-day plan focused on quick wins (vendor renegotiation, hiring freeze, prepayment incentives) while simultaneously executing structural improvements (pricing, productization, automation).”
- “We’ll track a short KPI dashboard and reforecast weekly. If runway deteriorates past 4 months, we will escalate to Board and consider financing or more significant reductions.”

8) Next steps / what I can build for you
I can:
- Build a 6–12 month cash & P&L model with scenario toggles (pricing, expense cuts, revenue growth) and a slide deck with the narrative above.
To do that I’ll need:
- Current monthly revenue breakdown (ARR / MRR, recurring vs one-time)
- Gross margin (or cost of goods/service) or variable costs
- Expense breakdown (payroll, marketing, cloud, rent, contractors, other)
- Major upcoming cash events (debt payments, vendor milestones)
- Planned hires & their monthly cost
- Any constraints (no layoffs, must preserve R&D, fundraising plans)

Would you like me to:
A) Build the 6–12 month model and slide deck (I’ll use reasonable defaults if you don’t have all the inputs), or
B) Draft a one-page finance narrative you can present right away?

Tell me which option and provide the items under “To do that I’ll need” if you choose A. If you prefer B, I’ll draft a tight one-page narrative + 5-slide deck outline now.

