Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — this is a clear starting point. I’ll (A) validate the basic math, (B) give focused, high-impact margin-improvement levers and scalability moves (with expected dollar effects and timing), (C) show a few simple runway / scenario calculations so you can see the leverage, and (D) provide a concise finance narrative you can use in an internal deck or memo. At the end I’ll list the data I’ll need to turn this into slides or a detailed model.

A — quick validation of the facts you gave
- Cash: $1.2M
- Burn: $195K / month
- Runway = cash / burn = 1,200,000 / 195,000 ≈ 6.15 months (matches your “6 months” statement)
- Revenue last quarter: $1.8M → monthly average ≈ $600K

B — prioritized levers for margin improvement & scalability (what to do, expected impact, owner, timeline)
Focus on 3 buckets: increase contribution margin (pricing/product mix/COGS), reduce operating burn (OPEX & headcount), and scale revenue efficiently.

1) Improve contribution/gross margin (high ROI, medium difficulty)
- Actions:
  - Reprice where you have pricing power (annual contracts, tiered pricing for premium features).
  - Push higher-margin products/services and bundle to raise average price.
  - Negotiate cost of goods sold / vendor fees (hosting, third-party licenses).
  - Reduce discounting and tighten SLA-based credits.
- Expected impact (example): A 5 percentage-point gross-margin lift on $600K/mo revenue = 0.05 × $600K = $30K/month additional gross margin.
- Timeline: 30–90 days for pricing and packaging changes; 60–120 days for vendor renegotiations.
- Owner: Product + Sales + Procurement.

2) Reduce operating burn (fast, direct impact)
- Actions:
  - Immediate hiring freeze + pause non-critical contractors.
  - Cut discretionary spend (travel, events, software duplications).
  - Consolidate or renegotiate major vendor contracts and leases.
  - Right-size marketing spend to higher-return channels.
- Expected impact (example): 10–25% reduction in non-headcount OPEX or total burn. If you target a 20% total burn reduction: 0.2 × $195K = $39K/month savings.
- Timeline: immediate (30 days) to implement and see full effect (1–2 months).
- Owner: Finance + HR + Department heads.

3) Revenue acceleration with efficient CAC / higher LTV (medium-term)
- Actions:
  - Focus sales on highest LTV accounts; tighten lead qualification.
  - Shorten sales cycles for net-new business (packaged offers, trials).
  - Expand upsell/cross-sell motions to existing customers.
  - Implement predictable pricing and renewals (annual prepay discounts).
- Expected impact: even a conservative 10% monthly revenue increase (to $660K/mo) structured into higher-margin contracts could add $6K–$30K+ incremental margin/month depending on contribution margin.
- Timeline: 60–180 days.
- Owner: Sales + Marketing + Customer Success.

4) Scalability / structural items (longer-term, high ROI)
- Actions:
  - Automate recurring processes (billing, onboarding, reporting).
  - Move to variable-cost models (cloud autoscaling, managed services) where it reduces fixed cost.
  - Modular teams aligned to product lines (so incremental revenue scales without linear headcount growth).
  - Standardize unit economics and measure CAC payback, gross margin by product.
- Timeline: 3–12 months.
- Owner: COO + Engineering + Finance.

C — simple scenario math (illustrative; we can refine with exact expense breakdown)

Base (current)
- Burn = $195K → runway ≈ 6.15 months

Scenario A — conservative (mix of margin lift + modest cut)
- 5 ppt gross-margin lift = +$30K/month
- 10% burn cut via ops = $19.5K/month
- Net burn = 195K − 30K − 19.5K = 145.5K/month
- Runway = 1.2M / 145.5K ≈ 8.25 months

Scenario B — targeted (bold but achievable)
- 5 ppt gross lift = +$30K
- 20% burn reduction = $39K
- Net burn = 195K − 30K − 39K = 126K
- Runway = 1.2M / 126K ≈ 9.5 months

Scenario C — aggressive growth + efficiency (mix of revenue growth and cuts)
- Revenue +10% → monthly rev = $660K; assume gross margin increases so incremental margin = $20K
- 15% burn cut = $29.25K
- Net burn = 195K − 20K − 29.25K = 145.75K
- Runway ≈ 1.2M / 145.75K ≈ 8.2 months

Key takeaway: modest margin and cost actions (5ppt margin improvement + ~15–20% OPEX cut) extend runway materially (from ~6 to ~8–9.5 months). Combined with even modest revenue growth the effect compounds.

D — concise finance narrative (ready to drop into a slide or memo)
Headline (one line)
- “Horizon Media: $1.2M cash, ~6 months runway. We will extend runway and accelerate profitable growth by improving margins, reducing discretionary spend, and prioritizing high-LTV revenue.”

Status / context (2–3 bullets)
- Cash: $1.2M; current burn $195K/month → runway ~6 months.
- Revenue last quarter: $1.8M (≈ $600K/month). Healthy topline but margin and expense mix create runway risk.

Strategy (what we will do, prioritized)
1. Short-term (0–90 days): immediate hiring freeze, cut discretionary spend, negotiate top 3 vendor contracts, implement pricing/discount discipline — expected to add 1–3 months of runway.
2. Medium-term (90–180 days): launch pricing/packaging changes, focus sales on top-tier accounts and upsell motions, automate onboarding/billing to reduce cost per sale and improve CAC payback.
3. Long-term (180+ days): re-architect cost structure to more variable costs, scale product lines with clear unit economics, and prepare a targeted raise if required.

Expected outcomes (quantified)
- A 5ppt gross margin lift + 20% burn reduction = ~+$69K/month net improvement → runway improves from ~6 months to ~9.5 months (illustrative).
- Aim: extend runway to ≥9 months while growing revenue, or secure a bridge round sized to reach clear profitability inflection.

Asks / decisions needed from leadership
- Approve 30-day hiring freeze and discretionary spend reduction.
- Approve vendor negotiation & target savings (e.g., 10–20% on major contracts).
- Approve short-term pricing test and limited sales incentives for upsell.
- Decide whether to pursue fundraise (size/timing) if runway <9 months post-actions.

KPIs to report weekly / monthly
- Cash balance, burn rate, runway (rolling).
- Gross margin % and contribution margin $/month.
- Revenue by cohort & by product; % of revenue from top 20 customers.
- CAC, payback period, churn and net expansion rate.
- Headcount and fixed vs variable cost split.

E — next steps and what I need from you to build a slide deck or model
If you want I will build a 1-page finance summary slide + a 3-scenario sensitivity (base / conservative / aggressive) and an Excel cash-burn model. To do that I need:
- Monthly expense breakdown (COGS, people, marketing, R&D, G&A) or last quarter P&L.
- Gross margin (or COGS) by product line if available.
- Customer concentration (top 5 customers % of revenue).
- Hiring plan and planned near-term commitments (offers, leases, contracts).
- Any fundraising timeline or target ranges (if already considering).

